There is real power in sunlight, but not in staring directly into the Sun.

Bright outdoor light hitting the eyes indirectly is a major biological timing signal: it helps set the circadian clock, shifts melatonin/cortisol timing, affects alertness and sleep, and entrains a lot of downstream physiology. In that sense, light is absolutely an input you can use to steer the body.

But direct sungazing is a bad control surface. The retina has no pain receptors, so solar retinal injury can occur without an immediate warning, and even sunrise/sunset viewing is not reliably safe. The useful signal saturates long before you need to stare at the solar disc.

So I’d separate the two:

sunlight exposure → powerful biological entrainment
directly staring at the Sun → unnecessary retinal risk

A better version of the practice is getting outside, especially after waking, with your eyes open normally and letting ambient sky light do the work—no need to look at the Sun itself.